# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
locke-treatise-3.md:1: argue/readings: S3 is read 3 ways: locke-treatise-3.logic.r2 has "distinct_governed" and "one_body ∧ in_nature" and "one_body ∧ in_nature → answers_natural" and "answers_natural → natural_power"; locke-treatise-3.logic.r2.2 has "answers_natural → natural_power" and "distinct_governed" and "one_body ∧ in_nature"; they differ when only "there is another power in every commonwealth which one may call natural", "in a commonwealth the members are distinct persons in reference to one another and are governed by the laws of the society", "in reference to the rest of mankind, the members of a commonwealth make one body" and "that body is still in the state of nature with the rest of mankind, as every member of it before was" are true, which the second allows; locke-treatise-3.logic.r2.3 has "distinct_governed" and "one_body ∧ in_nature"
locke-treatise-3.md:1: argue/answered: S3: answered no, which keeps locke-treatise-3.logic.r2, to: Suppose "there is another power in every commonwealth which one may call natural", "in a commonwealth the members are distinct persons in reference to one another and are governed by the laws of the society", "in reference to the rest of mankind, the members of a commonwealth make one body" and "that body is still in the state of nature with the rest of mankind, as every member of it before was" are true and "that power answers to the power every man naturally had before he entered into society" is false. Is S3, "There is another power in every commonwealth, which one may call natural, because it is that which answers to the power every man naturally had before he entered into society: for though in a commonwealth the members of it are distinct persons still in reference to one another, and as such as governed by the laws of the society; yet in reference to the rest of mankind, they make one body, which is, as every member of it before was, still in the state of nature with the rest of mankind.", true then?
locke-treatise-3.md:1: argue/readings: S4 is read 2 ways: locke-treatise-3.logic.r2 and locke-treatise-3.logic.r2.3 have nothing from it; locke-treatise-3.logic.r2.2 has "one_body ∧ in_nature → public_manages ∧ whole_repairs"; they differ when only "in reference to the rest of mankind, the members of a commonwealth make one body", "that body is still in the state of nature with the rest of mankind, as every member of it before was" and "controversies between any man of the society and those out of it are managed by the public" are true, which the first allows
locke-treatise-3.md:1: argue/answered: S4: answered no, which keeps locke-treatise-3.logic.r2.2, to: Suppose "in reference to the rest of mankind, the members of a commonwealth make one body", "that body is still in the state of nature with the rest of mankind, as every member of it before was" and "controversies between any man of the society and those out of it are managed by the public" are true and "an injury done to a member of the body engages the whole in the reparation of it" is false. Is S4, "Hence it is, that the controversies that happen between any man of the society with those that are out of it, are managed by the public; and an injury done to a member of their body, engages the whole in the reparation of it.", true then?
locke-treatise-3.logic.r2: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 3 ways: locke-treatise-3.logic.r2 has "one_body ∧ in_nature → public_manages ∧ whole_repairs"; locke-treatise-3.logic.r2.2 has nothing from it; they differ when only "in reference to the rest of mankind, the members of a commonwealth make one body", "that body is still in the state of nature with the rest of mankind, as every member of it before was" and "controversies between any man of the society and those out of it are managed by the public" are true, which the second allows; locke-treatise-3.logic.r2.3 has "one_body ∧ in_nature → answers_natural" and "answers_natural → natural_power" and "one_body ∧ in_nature → public_manages ∧ whole_repairs"
locke-treatise-3.logic.r2: argue/readings: U1: locke-treatise-3.logic.r2 and locke-treatise-3.logic.r2.3 take for granted "if (in reference to the rest of mankind, the members of a commonwealth make one body and that body is still in the state of nature with the rest of mankind, as every member of it before was), then that power answers to the power every man naturally had before he entered into society"; locke-treatise-3.logic.r2.2 does not
locke-treatise-3.logic.r2: argue/answered: U1: answered yes, which keeps locke-treatise-3.logic.r2 and locke-treatise-3.logic.r2.3, to: Does the text's argument take this for granted, whether or not it says so: "if (in reference to the rest of mankind, the members of a commonwealth make one body and that body is still in the state of nature with the rest of mankind, as every member of it before was), then that power answers to the power every man naturally had before he entered into society"? Answer yes if the author would accept it as part of the argument.
